Fillets: ChFi3d_FilBuilder::SplitSurf does not cut a blend at a minimum
of its section's width from which the sections stay narrower than its
singular tolerance on to an end of the line (mitcad#2390).

The case: the rim between a loft's side and a cylinder pulled from its end
face, split at the points where the faces are tangent, each piece's
rounding vanishing in a point at both ends. Rounded with 2, the last
sections of a walk into a vanishing end have no width (1e-7 to 1e-5 mm)
and minima of noise among them: SplitSurf, which cuts a blend into two
faces where its contacts meet inside it (a point the blend runs through,
mitcad#1420: within 100 times the approximation's 3d tolerance), cut the
blends 0.0002 before or after their vanishing ends into a piece and a
sliver of no width. With the top convex piece the build of the stripe
(ChFi3d_FilDS) then took the meeting of the two at the end vertex for the
spine going on with its next edge, read past the end of a spine of one
edge and crashed; with all the pieces at once the slivers' contacts of a
convex and a concave piece crossed at their common vertex, and both were
refused as overlapping; with 5 the bottom convex piece got a sliver face
and a volume 2600 mm3 off those with 2 and 10.

- ChFi3d_FilBuilder::SplitSurf: a minimum is taken only where the
  sections widen past that tolerance on both sides of it before the
  line's ends. A blend that narrows to a point and widens again is cut
  there as before.

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2303.
